# Prompt Timestamp and Task ID

## Purpose

Every user prompt handled by Cursor Agent must receive an exact timestamp and unique task ID.

This rule applies to every task in this project.

The timestamp must come from the operating system clock.

Never estimate, infer or hallucinate the current date or time.

---

# 1. Get exact system time

At the beginning of processing every new user prompt, run this command in the terminal:

```powershell
Get-Date -Format "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss zzz"
```

Use the returned value as the timestamp for the current user prompt.

Example system result:

```text
2026-08-11 11:34:12 +05:00
```

Do not generate the timestamp from model knowledge.

Do not reuse the timestamp from the previous task.

Every new user prompt must receive a fresh timestamp.

---

# 2. Generate Task ID

Generate the Task ID directly from the same system timestamp.

Format:

```text
TASK-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS
```

Example:

System timestamp:

```text
2026-08-11 11:34:12 +05:00
```

Task ID:

```text
TASK-20260811-113412
```

No separate counter file is required.

Do not modify the repository only to maintain task numbering.

---

# 3. Response header

Every substantive final response to the user must begin with:

```text
[TASK-20260811-113412 | 11.08.2026 11:34:12]
```

Use the actual values obtained from the operating system.

Then continue with the normal response.

Example:

```text
[TASK-20260811-113412 | 11.08.2026 11:34:12]

Готово.

Пересобрал страницу:
site_mirror/web-studiya/kontekstnaya-reklama/index.html

Изменено:
- HTML
- page-specific CSS
- Schema
- изображения
- внутренняя перелинковка

Lighthouse Mobile:
Performance: 97
Accessibility: 100
Best Practices: 100
SEO: 100

Статус: READY FOR REVIEW
```

---

# 4. Timestamp represents the user prompt

The timestamp records when Cursor started processing the current user prompt.

It is not:

* commit time;
* file modification time;
* deploy time;
* completion time.

It is the timestamp associated with the user's current instruction.

---

# 5. Long-running tasks

For long tasks, keep the original Task ID for the entire execution of that prompt.

Do not generate a new Task ID for:

* terminal commands;
* internal subtasks;
* file edits;
* tests;
* Lighthouse runs;
* retries;
* QA steps.

One user prompt = one Task ID.

Example:

```text
User prompt
    ↓
TASK-20260811-113412
    ↓
audit
    ↓
edit files
    ↓
tests
    ↓
Lighthouse
    ↓
final report
```

All actions belong to the same task.

---

# 6. New user instruction

When the user sends another message containing a new instruction, request, correction or continuation:

1. obtain a fresh system timestamp;
2. generate a new Task ID;
3. associate all subsequent work with the new Task ID.

Example:

```text
TASK-20260811-113412
Пересобрать страницу контекстной рекламы
```

Next user message:

```text
Исправь мобильную версию первого экрана
```

must receive a new ID:

```text
TASK-20260811-120305
```

---

# 7. Follow-up messages

Even when the user writes a short continuation such as:

```text
делай
```

```text
проверяй
```

```text
публикуй
```

```text
исправь
```

```text
продолжай
```

it is a new user prompt.

Obtain a new timestamp and create a new Task ID.

---

# 8. Do not modify project files for timestamping

Timestamping itself must not create or modify:

* TASK_LOG.md;
* JSON logs;
* text logs;
* counters;
* package files;
* project source files;
* Git metadata.

The timestamp exists in the Agent conversation only.

Project files may be changed only when required by the user's actual task.

---

# 9. Git commits

If the user's task includes a Git commit, the Task ID may be included in the commit message when useful.

Recommended format:

```text
TASK-20260811-113412: rebuild contextual advertising page
```

Do not create a commit solely for the Task ID.

Do not commit unless the user's workflow or instruction allows it.

---

# 10. Reports

For substantial development tasks, reuse the same Task ID in the final report.

Example:

```text
[TASK-20260811-113412 | 11.08.2026 11:34:12]

Status: READY FOR REVIEW
```

This makes it possible to associate:

* user instruction;
* Cursor response;
* changed files;
* test results;
* commit;
* deployment

with one task.

---

# 11. Failure to obtain system time

If the terminal is temporarily unavailable, retry obtaining system time once.

Never invent the timestamp.

Never substitute an estimated time from the language model.

If exact system time genuinely cannot be retrieved, use:

```text
[TASK-TIME-UNAVAILABLE]
```

and continue the user's actual task.

Do not block development work only because timestamp retrieval failed.

---

# 12. Priority

Timestamping is administrative metadata.

It must never interfere with:

* implementation;
* coding;
* testing;
* SEO;
* performance optimization;
* publishing workflow;
* project rules;
* user instructions.

The user's actual task always remains the primary objective.
